Spinal cord injury
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Loss of motor function below lesion, Absent sensory perception, Bowel/bladder incontinence, Autonomic dysreflexia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-046
Reason for Visit: Acute severe headache, profound hypertension, and suspected autonomic dysreflexia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a known T4 spinal cord injury who suddenly developed a pounding headache, extreme facial flushing, and a blood pressure reading of 190/110 mmHg on their home monitor over the past 30 minutes. Assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period due to the critical, immediate risk of intracranial haemorrhage or myocardial infarction secondary to an uncontrolled autonomic dysreflexia crisis, requiring immediate clinical intervention to identify and remove the noxious stimulus.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My head is pounding out of my skull, I am sweating profusely above my chest, and I feel a terrible sense of panic.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 30-minute history of acute, severe, throbbing headache and profuse diaphoresis above the nipple line. The patient has a known history of a complete T4 spinal cord injury resulting in sudden and complete loss of voluntary motor function and absent sensory perception below the lesion. Due to the high thoracic lesion, they are at chronic risk for autonomic dysreflexia. The current episode escalated rapidly. The caregiver notes the patient has established complete loss of bowel and bladder sphincter control and relies on an indwelling suprapubic catheter, which currently appears to have low output for the past 4 hours.
Functional Status: Wheelchair-dependent; requires moderate assistance for transfers and bowel/bladder management.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate life-threatening risk of hypertensive stroke.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 195/115 mmHg | HR: 52 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78.0 Kg
General: Patient is in acute distress, visibly flushed and diaphoretic above the mid-chest, with compensatory piloerection (goosebumps) and pallor below the lesion level.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals profound paroxysmal hypertension and compensatory bradycardia (HR 52). Neurological exam confirms established flaccid paralysis and absent sensory perception below the T4 dermatome. Abdominal exam reveals a firm, distended, palpable bladder extending midway to the umbilicus. The indwelling suprapubic catheter is mechanically kinked beneath the patient's clothing, obstructing all outflow. No signs of acute bony localized spinal pain or tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Wheelchair cushion is appropriately inflated; no obvious external pressure sores noted during a brief visual sweep.
Medication Reconciliation: PRN sublingual Captopril is prescribed for these emergencies but was unable to be located by the caregiver.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Spinal cord injury (complicated by acute autonomic dysreflexia secondary to catheter occlusion) - Status: Acute, critical complication
Differentials:

Essential Hypertensive Crisis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of profound bradycardia and the sharp anatomical demarcation of flushing/sweating, which is the pathognomonic reflex response in high spinal lesions.

Acute Migraine - Ruled out/unlikely due to the sudden onset coinciding with a massively distended bladder, the lack of photophobia, and the objective extreme blood pressure elevation.

Sepsis (e.g., severe UTI) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary current driver, because there is no fever and the acute crisis is purely hemodynamic (autonomic), though a UTI may be brewing behind the obstruction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Medical emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook, life-threatening crisis of autonomic dysreflexia. The objective findings of a blood pressure of 195/115 mmHg, reflex bradycardia, and diaphoresis distinctly above the T4 lesion in the presence of a mechanically kinked catheter and distended bladder confirmed the diagnosis. This assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period because the unopposed sympathetic surge below the lesion causes massive vasoconstriction, placing the patient at immediate risk for a fatal hemorrhagic stroke. Rapid in-person mechanical relief of the obstructed catheter was mandatory to instantly eliminate the noxious stimulus and abort the hypertensive crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately elevated the head of the bed to 90 degrees to utilize orthostatic pooling to lower intracranial pressure. Located and unkinked the suprapubic catheter tubing, resulting in an immediate rush of 800mL of clear urine. Blood pressure was re-assessed every 5 minutes and dropped to a safe baseline of 115/70 mmHg within 15 minutes, aborting the crisis without needing pharmacological intervention.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you visibly trace the entire length of the catheter tubing with your hand after every single transfer, you ensure it is not pinched; therefore, making this manual check a strict habit prevents these life-threatening pressure spikes. When you keep the emergency blood pressure medication permanently taped to the side of the wheelchair, it is instantly available; ensuring this placement guarantees rapid response if an obstruction cannot be immediately found.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to the spinal outreach nursing team to review catheter management techniques.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended the use of a rigid protective casing for the exposed section of the suprapubic tube.
Follow-up: Telehealth GP review tomorrow to ensure blood pressure remains stable and to replace the expired emergency Captopril.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the pounding headache returns and your blood pressure goes above 160 systolic, if you cannot find and fix a tubing kink immediately, or if you develop sudden weakness or numbness in your face or arms.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Autonomic dysreflexia is a lifelong risk for high spinal cord injuries; meticulous prevention of noxious stimuli is the primary treatment.
Clinical Course: The acute crisis is fully resolved following bladder decompression. A mild residual headache may persist for a few hours as the cerebral vasculature normalizes.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The caregiver must monitor the patient's blood pressure every 2 hours for the remainder of the evening. Seek urgent care if the catheter stops draining again or if the urine becomes cloudy and foul-smelling.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because your spinal cord is injured, your brain cannot send the signal to relax the blood vessels in your lower body when your bladder gets painfully full. This caused a massive pressure backup into your head. Unkinking the tube removed the pain signal, allowing your blood pressure to instantly return to normal.
Actionable Steps: Always trace the catheter tubing after moving. Tape the emergency medication to the wheelchair. Monitor your blood pressure closely tonight.

